105	     the tags and attributes in the input HTML are allowed by the safelist, and no tag, attribute, or attribute value
106	     would be removed or rewritten by the cleaner. The input is parsed as an HTML body fragment and is not modified;
107	     malformed URLs are treated as invalid, without throwing.
108	     <p>
109	     This method applies the same safety checks as {@link #clean(Document)}, so its result always reflects the current
110	     safelist configuration. It is intended to be used in a user interface as a validator for user input. Note that
111	     regardless of the output of this method, the input HTML <b>must always</b> be normalized using a method such as
112	     {@link #clean(Document)}, and the result of that method used to store or serialize the document before later
113	     reuse such as presentation to end users. This ensures that enforced attributes are set correctly, and that any
114	     differences between how a given browser and how jsoup parses the input HTML are normalized.
115	     </p>
116	     <p>Example:
117	     <pre>{@code
118	     Safelist safelist = Safelist.relaxed();
119	     boolean isValid = Cleaner.isValid(inputHtml, safelist);
120	     String normalizedHtml = Jsoup.clean(inputHtml, "https://example.com/", safelist);
121	     }</pre>
122	     </p>
